<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
    <artifactId>eyeandsickle-parent</artifactId>
    <version>${revision}</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>eyeandsickle-client</artifactId>
  <name>The Eye and Sickle :: Client</name>
  <description>
    The cross-platform JavaFX desktop client (docs/architecture/01 §1).

    Multi-window by design: one javafx.stage.Stage per tool — map, terminal, rig
    monitor, recon — so the player's screen ends up looking like an operator's
    desk (docs/design/05 §5). A single-window docked fallback is REQUIRED for
    accessibility, not optional (docs/architecture/01 §1, flagged for review).

    The client is a view and input layer. It is never authoritative over
    anything a cheater would forge (Invariant I14). It may mirror server state
    for prediction and it may re-verify provenance chains offline against DID
    public keys (docs/architecture/04 §6.2) — but it decides nothing.
  </description>

  <properties>
    <client.main.class>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle.client.Launcher</client.main.class>
    <javafx.app.class>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle.client.EyeAndSickleClient</javafx.app.class>
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
      <artifactId>eyeandsickle-protocol</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      The single-player runtime. This is what lets the game start with no
      internet, no account and no PostgreSQL.

      It is NOT a back door around the enforcer rules below. `solo` is a plain
      library with no Spring, no driver and no HTTP stack (its own pom enforces
      that), and a character it creates is local-only and can never federate —
      so nothing it produces can enter the economy I14 protects. See
      solo/pom.xml for the full argument.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle</groupId>
      <artifactId>eyeandsickle-solo</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      JavaFX platform natives resolve automatically: org.openjfx's parent pom
      carries profiles keyed on os.name/os.arch that set ${javafx.platform} to
      mac-aarch64 / mac / linux / linux-aarch64 / win, and each artifact pulls
      its own classifier. No os-maven-plugin needed for local development.
      Building for a different OS than the one you are on means pinning the
      classifier explicitly; see the packaging note at the bottom of this file.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
      <artifactId>javafx-controls</artifactId>
      <version>${javafx.version}</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
      <artifactId>javafx-fxml</artifactId>
      <version>${javafx.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Native-feeling theming across macOS / Linux / Windows so the client
         doesn't read as a 2005 Swing app on any of them. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.mkpaz</groupId>
      <artifactId>atlantafx-base</artifactId>
      <version>${atlantafx.version}</version>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.openjfx</groupId>
        <artifactId>javafx-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <mainClass>${javafx.app.class}</mainClass>
          <options>
            <!--
              JavaFX loads native graphics libraries via System::load, which JDK
              24+ reports as a restricted-method call and which a future JDK will
              block outright. Granting it explicitly silences the warning now and
              stops the client from breaking on a JDK bump later.
            -->
            <option>--enable-native-access=javafx.graphics</option>
          </options>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <archive>
            <manifest>
              <mainClass>${client.main.class}</mainClass>
            </manifest>
          </archive>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>client-is-not-authoritative</id>
            <goals><goal>enforce</goal></goals>
            <configuration>
              <rules>
                <bannedDependencies>
                  <message>
                    INVARIANT I14. The client must not depend on the server
                    module or on Spring, and must never reach a database driver.
                    Server code on the client classpath is how "the client
                    decided the outcome" bugs start. Talk to the server over
                    REST/WebSocket using types from eyeandsickle-protocol.

                    The absence of a &lt;dependency&gt; element is not
                    self-defending — someone will add one. This rule is.
                  </message>
                  <searchTransitive>true</searchTransitive>
                  <excludes>
                    <exclude>io.github.stoicswe.eyeandsickle:eyeandsickle-server</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.springframework:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.springframework.boot:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.postgresql:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.flywaydb:*</exclude>
                    <exclude>org.hibernate*:*</exclude>
                  </excludes>
                </bannedDependencies>
              </rules>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

  <!--
    NO `dist` PROFILE YET — deliberately, and this is worth reading before you
    add one back.

    docs/architecture/01 §1 lists self-contained packaging (jlink/jpackage) as a
    [PROPOSAL] so players never have to "install a JRE first". An earlier draft
    of this pom wired `javafx:jlink` into a `dist` profile. It does not work, and
    it fails for a structural reason rather than a configuration mistake:

      jlink can only link EXPLICIT JPMS modules. This client runs from the
      classpath and has no module-info.java, and — more stubbornly — the
      provenance canonicalizer `io.github.erdtman:java-json-canonicalization`
      is a plain Java 8 jar with no module descriptor, so it can only ever be
      an AUTOMATIC module, and jlink refuses to link automatic modules.

    So the two real options, whenever this is picked up:

      1. jpackage in non-modular app-image mode (`type app-image`, `input <dir>`, `main-jar`). Works today with the classpath layout, produces a bundled
         runtime, needs no module-info anywhere. Largest image, least friction.
         This is the recommended first move.
      2. Full JPMS: add module-info.java to protocol and client AND replace or
         patch the canonicalizer with something modular. Smallest image, but it
         is a real dependency-graph project, not an afternoon.

    Shipping a profile whose documented command fails every time is worse than
    shipping no profile, so there is no profile. `mvn verify` and `mvn
    -pl client javafx:run` are the supported paths until this is built properly.
  -->
</project>
